      "message": "feat(tools): align a vendor STEP by ISO 13399 tool coordinate systems\n\nWire the ISO 13399 coordinate system reader into the tool-body pipeline\nso vendor tool STEP packages carrying named coordinate systems align\nautomatically without manual numeric offsets.\n\n- ToolCsysReport::align_tool_body() extracts the tool axis (from CIP\n  or CSIF) and calculates the tip offset (-dot(origin, axis)) so that\n  the cutting reference point sits at Z = 0 in the tool frame, pointing\n  up into the spindle along +Z.\n- ToolBodySpec::from_step_auto(&path) reads both mesh facets and coordinate\n  systems, aligning with no manual offsets.\n- SetToolBody::from_step_auto(&world, tool_name, &path) attaches the auto-aligned\n  tool body as one undoable command.\n- tool_body_view::load_aligned(&path) returns LoadedBody carrying the auto-alignment\n  result or fallback reason, preserving load backwards compatibility.\n- In the tool editor (ToolBodyUi), importing a STEP file with ISO 13399 coordinate\n  systems automatically sets the axis and tip offset with the note\n  'aligned by ISO 13399 coordinate systems'; files without them or with\n  degenerate/ambiguous coordinate systems fall back to default numeric placement\n  ([0, 0, 1] axis, 0 offset) with a note naming why.\n- Test fixture testdata/models/tool_csys_box_iso13399.step combining a B-rep box\n  and ISO 13399 placements.\n- docs/decisions/370-tool-csys-align.md.\n\nFork synced at v0.16.0.\n\nChangelog: Tool bodies: align a vendor STEP by its ISO 13399 tool coordinate systems (#370)\n\nFixes #370",
